Title: Nick_and_Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - EVENING  
A cramped fifth-floor walk-up in Milwaukee. Faded posters on the wall and mismatched furniture set the tone of a troubled, working-class home.  
NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Big brother was mine. Nick – the guy everyone was scared of – was my protector and my curse. He carried a pair of steel wrecking balls for arms, and his rough, patchworked face told stories of brawls and bruised pride.  
On a worn couch, the NARRATOR, a thoughtful young man with tired eyes, sits beside MA, a gentle woman with tired but kind features.  
MA  
(half-whisper)  
Your brother’s gonna come in any minute now…  
NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Ma and I managed somehow after Dad left. I was stuck balancing groceries and responsibility, while Nick made the impossible look effortless.  

CUT TO:

EXT. MILWAUKEE SIDEWALK - NIGHT  
The rain slicks the uneven pavement as the NARRATOR pedals home on his bike, remembering past trips to the store with Nick hauling bags in one mighty stride.  
NARRATOR (V.O.)  
I held the rest. Every errand was a lesson in dependence—and independence.

SMASH TO:

INT. LIBRARY - LATE AFTERNOON  
Rows of ancient books in warm lamplight. The NARRATOR wanders, searching for refuge among classic literature. He stops in the “Classic Literature” section.  
NARRATOR  
(quietly, to himself)  
Wuthering Heights… the story of Heathcliff and Catherine.  
A young woman with kind, light-blue eyes, SIONNA, stands nearby examining a book. Their eyes meet.  
NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Then, as if destiny brushed past me, I met Sionna.  
NARRATOR  
(approaching gently)  
Are you okay?  
SIONNA  
(smiling shyly)  
Yes… I’m Sionna.  
NARRATOR  
I work here—and I was wondering, do you want to grab a cup of coffee sometime?  
SIONNA  
(with matter-of-fact ease)  
Maybe. I do love discussing books… and ideas.

CUT TO:

INT. THE JAVA HUT - AFTERNOON  
A small, hip coffee shop with eclectic décor. Sionna sits at a table near the back; the NARRATOR approaches, nervous but intrigued.  
NARRATOR  
(softly)  
Wuthering Heights… I picked it out last night.  
SIONNA  
(smiling, lively)  
I know it well. I’ve always found the doomed passion of Heathcliff strangely liberating.  
They engage in animated conversation—philosophy, dreams that mix past lives with present desires—intercut by gentle laughter and shared glances.  
NARRATOR (V.O.)  
She challenged me to think, to feel—like I could finally glimpse the better parts of myself.

CUT TO:

INT. JOE’S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT  
A rough bar filled with boisterous chatter and clinking glasses. The NARRATOR sits nursing a beer when NICK, an imposing, scarred figure with wild hair, bursts in.  
NICK  
(slapping the NARRATOR on the back)  
Hey, little brother. What’s shakin’?  
NARRATOR  
(avoiding conflict)  
Just trying to keep Ma happy...  
The tension mounts. NICK’s voice grows harsh as he berates the NARRATOR.  
NICK  
Ma’s been sleeping all day! You gotta keep an eye on her, or she’s gonna turn into some demented old fart!  
NARRATOR  
(defensive)  
I promised I’d take her out tomorrow.  
A volatile silence. The bar’s noise seems to swell as Nick’s eyes flash with anger.  
NICK (CONT’D)  
(under his breath, almost a threat)  
You’re always messing up, little brother.
NARRATOR (V.O.)  
In that moment, I felt torn—torn between loyalty and the need to break free.

CUT TO:

INT. APARTMENT LIVING ROOM - LATE NIGHT  
Later that evening. The NARRATOR returns home to find Ma confused and agitated near a stove.  
MA  
(frantic)  
I can’t find my pain pills... Did we go to church today?  
NARRATOR  
(rushing to comfort her)  
Your pills are right here, Ma… I’ll take care of it.  
His worry is palpable. He makes sure Ma is safe, then slips into a solitude where the weight of responsibility crashes over him.  
NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Managing Ma, handling my own heart—everything smeared together like the crust of an old pizza.

DISSOLVE TO:

INT. HOSPITAL WAITING AREA - AFTERNOON  
A sterile, hushed room. The NARRATOR sits alone, phone in hand, listening to a trembling message from DR. CARSON.  
DR. CARSON (V.O. on phone)  
Your mother’s blood pressure is dangerously high. We need to admit her immediately.  
NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Fear and guilt wrapped around me as I realized the fragile thread holding Ma’s life.

CUT TO:

INT. COURTROOM – DAY  
A modest courtroom scene. The NARRATOR, looking worn and disheartened, stands before a judge. His lawyer, DANIEL DAWSON, speaks in measured tones as the courtroom listens.  
DANIEL DAWSON  
Your actions, though unintentional, contributed to the tragedy. Involuntary manslaughter charges are pending.  
The NARRATOR’s face is a mask of remorse and numb acceptance as he grapples with the consequence of a drunk driving accident that claimed Ma’s life.  
NARRATOR (V.O.)  
I was drowning in legal and moral guilt. Every misstep echoed Ma’s absence.

CUT TO:

INT. PRISON VISIT ROOM - DAY  
A sterile, small room with a thick plexiglass barrier. The NARRATOR sits on one side. On the opposite side, NICK, now gaunt and remorseful behind prison bars, stares back.  
NICK  
(hoarse, regretful)  
Little brother... I—  
NARRATOR  
(interrupting, voice trembling with both anger and sorrow)  
I’m here to tell you, Nick, that even though you hurt me—hurt Ma—and ruined so many lives, I have to let go of the anger. I forgive you.  
A long, pained silence follows as tears stream down NICK’s face.  
NICK  
(whispering)  
I…I’m so sorry. I never meant to destroy everything.  
NARRATOR (V.O.)  
In that moment, forgiveness became my salvation—a choice to free myself from the chains of the past.

CUT TO:

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY  
A bright, modern kitchen inside a bustling pizzeria in Seattle. The NARRATOR, now more confident and focused, oversees preparations alongside a team. A neon sign reads “TONY’S BOY’S PIZZA.”  
NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Time, as always, marched on. I transformed pain into purpose—owning my own pizza chain, forging a future far beyond Milwaukee’s brick walls.  
A friendly BOB, a loyal employee, exchanges a warm nod with the NARRATOR as they plate fresh pizzas.  
BOB  
(cheerfully)  
You really did it, boss. You turned your life around.  
NARRATOR  
(smiling)  
I had to eventually let go of the past... and embrace the future.

CUT TO:

INT. SMALL MEDITATION ROOM IN THE APARTMENT - NIGHT  
A peaceful, softly lit room lined with candles and simple décor. The NARRATOR sits cross-legged on a cushion. His eyes are closed; his face serene as he breathes deeply.  
NARRATOR (V.O.)  
I learned that healing isn’t the absence of pain, but the acceptance of it—and the courage to transform it into something meaningful.  
A memory of SIONNA’s gentle smile and encouraging words echoes in his mind.  
SIONNA (V.O.)  
Love is the universal language. It frees us when we forgive, and it teaches us our true purpose.  
The NARRATOR opens his eyes, a quiet calm etched across his features.  
NARRATOR (V.O.)  
I forgave. I grew. I found peace at last.

FADE OUT / THE END
